SUMMARY:Echoes of the Earth
DESCRIPTION:65 min | English with subtitles | India | Ganesh Shankar Raj \nEchoes of the Earth: Indigenous Knowledge and Climate Resilience in Karnataka and Kerala explores how traditional knowledge\, shaped by centuries of observation\, can build climate resilience and nurture a careful\, sustainable relationship with the environment. \nThrough personal stories and intimate portraits of everyday life\, the film highlights practices that conserve forests\, biodiversity and natural resources. It also reflects on how climate change is already affecting plants\, wildlife and ecosystems\, particularly among the Soliga communities of Karnataka. What can these knowledge systems teach us about living sustainably\, and what might be overlooked when their ecological dimensions are viewed mainly through cultural or religious traditions? \nThe screening will be followed by a panel discussion on the role of Indigenous women as knowledge holders and contributors to environmental stewardship. Indigenous knowledge is not only a legacy of the past\, but a living resource for building climate resilience today. \nHigh Tea will be served from 6 pm onwards. \nPresented by: \n \n \nVenue: Auditorium\n\n\nIndigenous Knowledge and Climate Resilience in Karnataka and Kerala\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc5L13wGxOoe30BNBgO3y7UEwrv1y9ncnzpb3IfyUdAEPZ1AQ/viewform?usp=pp_url)\n*Speakers*: R. Siddappa Setty\, M. Ushashree\, Seema Purushothaman\n*Event Categories*: Climate Change\, Environment\, Film\, Society\, Sustainability
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/echoes-of-the-earth/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Climate Change,Environment,Film,Society,Sustainability
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Thumbnail_EOTE.jpg
END:VEVENT
